{"model": "qwen3-coder:30b-a3b-q4_K_M", "created_at": "2026-09-17T16:05:34.364041946Z", "response": "Let's go through each problem one by one.\n\n---\n\n**Problem 1:**  \nMaria buys 7 notebooks at $4 each and 12 pens at $1.50 each.  \nCost of notebooks = 7 \u00d7 $4 = $28  \nCost of pens = 12 \u00d7 $1.50 = $18  \nTotal cost before discount = $28 + $18 = $46  \nDiscount = 10% of $46 = 0.10 \u00d7 $46 = $4.60  \nAmount paid after discount = $46 - $4.60 = $41.40  \n\nANSWER 1: 41.40\n\n---\n\n**Problem 2:**  \nIn 4 years, Ben will be 30 \u21d2 Ben is currently 26 years old.  \nBen is twice as old as Cara was 6 years ago \u21d2 Let Cara\u2019s current age = C  \nThen, 26 = 2 \u00d7 (C - 6)  \n26 = 2C - 12  \n38 = 2C  \nC = 19  \n\nANSWER 2: 19\n\n---\n\n**Problem 3:**  \nWe are given:\n- Alice does not own the fish.\n- Dog owner drinks coffee.\n- Ben drinks juice.\n- Cleo does not drink tea.\n\nSince Ben drinks juice, he is not the one who drinks coffee \u21d2 Ben doesn't own the dog.  \nThe dog owner drinks coffee \u21d2 so the dog owner must be either Alice or Cleo.  \nCleo does not drink tea \u21d2 she can only drink juice or coffee.  \nBut Ben drinks juice \u21d2 Cleo doesn\u2019t drink juice \u21d2 Cleo drinks coffee \u21d2 Cleo owns the dog.  \nSince Alice does not own the fish, and Cleo owns the dog, Alice must own the cat.  \nSo Ben owns the fish.\n\nThus:\n- Dog owner: Cleo\n- Ben's pet: fish\n\nANSWER 3: Cleo owns the dog; Ben owns the fish.\n\n---\n\n**Problem 4:**  \nSequence: 2, 6, 12, 20, 30, ?  \nLet\u2019s look at differences:\n- 6 - 2 = 4\n- 12 - 6 = 6\n- 20 - 12 = 8\n- 30 - 20 = 10\n\nDifferences increase by 2 each time: 4, 6, 8, 10 \u21d2 Next difference = 12  \nSo next term = 30 + 12 = 42  \n\nANSWER 4: 42\n\n---\n\n**Problem 5:**  \nFarmer has 17 sheep. All but 9 die \u21d2 9 survive.  \n\nANSWER 5: 9\n\n---\n\n**Problem 6:**  \nPipe A fills the tank in 6 hours \u21d2 rate = 1/6 per hour  \nPipe B fills the tank in 3 hours \u21d2 rate = 1/3 per hour  \nCombined rate = 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/6 + 2/6 = 3/6 = 1/2 per hour  \nTime to fill = 1 \u00f7 (1/2) = 2 hours  \n\nANSWER 6: 2\n\n---\n\n**Problem 7:**  \nA kilogram of feathers = 1 kg  \nA pound of steel = ~0.45 kg  \nSo, a kilogram is heavier than a pound.\n\nANSWER 7: A kilogram of feathers\n\n---\n\n**Problem 8:**  \nLet original cost = $100  \nRetail price = 100 + 25% = $125  \nSale discount = 20% off $125 = 0.20 \u00d7 $125 = $25  \nFinal sale price = $125 - $25 = $100  \nSo, sale price = original cost \u21d2 100%\n\nANSWER 8: 100%\n\n---\n\n**Problem 9:**  \nX says: \u201cAt least one of us is a liar.\u201d  \nIf X is telling the truth \u21d2 then at least one of them (X or Y) is a liar. Since X speaks truth, Y must be the liar.  \nIf X were lying \u21d2 then it's false that \"at least one of us is a liar.\" That would mean both are truth-tellers. But then X couldn't lie \u2014 contradiction.\n\nSo X must be a truth-teller and Y must be a liar.\n\nANSWER 9: X is a truth-teller; Y is a liar.\n\n---\n\n**Problem 10:**  \n3x + 7 = 2x + 15  \nSubtract 2x from both sides: x + 7 = 15  \nSubtract 7 from both sides: x = 8  \n\nANSWER 10: 8\n\n---\n\n**Problem 11:**  \nChoose 3 people from 5 \u21d2 Use combination formula  \nC(5,3) = 5! / (3!(5 - 3)!) = 5 \u00d7 4 / (2 \u00d7 1) = 10  \n\nANSWER 11: 10\n\n---\n\n**Problem 12:**  \nGiven:\n- Dan > Ellie  \n- Ellie < Farah \u21d2 Farah > Ellie  \n- Farah < Dan \u21d2 Dan > Farah  \nCombining: Dan > Farah > Ellie  \nSo, tallest = Dan; shortest = Ellie  \n\nANSWER 12: Dan is tallest; Ellie is shortest.\n\n---", "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "context": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 678, 220, 16, 17, 5322, 3685, 13, 6928, 697, 975, 421, 498, 1366, 11, 714, 369, 94413, 3491, 11, 6248, 448, 198, 64, 1555, 304, 4063, 6823, 8932, 419, 3561, 320, 603, 817, 3491, 11, 304, 1973, 11, 518, 279, 1602, 835, 315, 697, 2033, 7731, 11692, 39351, 220, 16, 25, 366, 21732, 4226, 397, 11692, 39351, 220, 17, 25, 366, 21732, 4226, 397, 9338